// VoxelFluidOccupancy.usf -- the builder for the PBF collision volume.
//
// Fills the 512^3 occupancy bitfield VoxelFluidContract.ush:64-69 describes,
// one dirty box at a time, from packed brick solidity the host uploads. The
// READER is VoxelFluidCollision.ush and it is the only reader; this is the
// only writer. Phase 0 spike (c) of
// docs/water-rearchitecture-plan-2026-08-09.md.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE SOLIDITY COMES FROM, AND WHY NOT FROM VoxelizeMain
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The obvious source is the GPU worldgen chain's OutCells (worldgen.ush:2582-
// 2596, one uint material per cell) -- occupancy would be a reduction of a
// grid the mesher already computes. It was investigated first and rejected for
// three reasons, in ascending order of how expensive the mistake would be:
//
//   1. NO RESIDENCY. OutCells is an RDG transient created inside
//      AddRegionPasses (VoxelGpuWorldGen.cpp:612-614) and gone when the graph
//      executes. Nothing keeps it. Occupancy would have to re-dispatch the
//      whole ColumnMain -> VoxelizeMain chain, raster upload included, for
//      terrain that is already meshed and resident -- 4,096 chunk regions to
//      cover one 51.2 m volume, none of which need re-meshing.
//
//   2. IT IS BLIND TO EDITS, BY CONSTRUCTION. A chunk containing (or merely
//      bordering) an edited brick is never GPU-meshed: it takes the
//      overlay-aware game-thread path
//      (FVoxelWorldImpl::ChunkHasEditedBrick, VoxelWorldSubsystem.cpp:6783-
//      6814). Worldgen output is the pure generated function; the edit overlay
//      is applied on the CPU by World::materialAt (voxelcore/world.h:46-53).
//      So sourcing occupancy from VoxelizeMain would be wrong exactly where
//      the plan's own scenario tests live -- "dig a channel from a river",
//      "breach a lake into a valley" (plan, Verification). Water would flow
//      through the trench walls and over the dug channel floor.
//
//   3. IT WOULD DRAG THE FLUID INTO THE WORLDGEN VERSION LOCK. worldgen.ush is
//      compiled two ways and pinned by a determinism digest, and new kernels
//      are deliberately kept out of it (VoxelQuadPoolWrite.usf:41-48;
//      VoxelFluidContract.ush:13-15 says the same about this contract). A
//      fluid kernel inside it could not be touched without a SPIR-V respin.
//
// SO THE SOURCE IS THE MESHER'S OWN SOURCE: vxc::World, overlay included --
// the same brickAt() the game-thread mesher reads. The host packs each brick's
// solidity to 1 bit with vxc::packBrickSolidBits
// (voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h) and uploads 64 bytes per brick instead of 2 KB
// of materials. Two consequences worth stating out loud:
//
//   * The GPU never sees a material id. isSolidForFluid lives in ONE place, on
//     the CPU, and there is no HLSL mirror of the material enum to drift.
//   * A full 512^3 volume is 16 MB of bits either way; as materials it would
//     be 512 MB. The bit packing is what makes region uploads affordable at
//     all.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY THIS IS A COMPUTE PASS AND NOT A CPU-SIDE MEMCPY
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The host has BRICKS (8^3 blocks, bits in brick-local order); the volume
// wants X-MAJOR words spanning 32 voxels along x -- four bricks per word. Doing
// that transpose on the CPU means keeping a 16 MB system-memory shadow of the
// volume and then uploading a dirty box as a scatter of 64-byte row fragments
// through RHI locks. That is precisely the render-thread lock/memcpy pattern
// Wave D spent a wave removing (VoxelQuadPoolWrite.usf:28-40), for bytes the
// GPU would end up owning anyway. Here the upload is ONE contiguous buffer of
// brick bits and the transpose is 64 threads' worth of shifts.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ALIGNMENT: WHY REGIONS ARE SNAPPED RATHER THAN CLAMPED
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One output word is 32 voxels of x, owned by exactly one thread, written
// whole. If a region began part-way through a word the kernel would need a
// read-modify-write, and two regions dispatched into the same graph could then
// race on the shared word -- a data race whose symptom is a few stray solid
// voxels near a chunk border, which is unfindable. So the host snaps every
// region OUTWARD to 32 voxels in x and 8 (one brick) in y and z
// (vxc::fluidSnapRegion), and every write here is a full word with no atomics
// and no ordering between regions. The cost is at most three extra brick
// columns per region, and the host can always produce them.
//
// THE WHOLE-VOLUME CLEAR IS NOT HERE. A clear of the entire 16 MiB to "all
// solid" is AddClearUAVPass on the host side (VoxelFluidOccupancy.cpp) -- RDG
// already has a clear and a kernel that only stores a constant would be a
// second mechanism for it. All-solid rather than all-air is the "blocked, never
// guessed" rule: an unbuilt volume freezes particles, it does not leak them.
//
// A SUB-BOX clear is a different question and FluidOccupancyMarkUnbuiltMain
// below is the answer to it: RDG has no ranged buffer clear, and the only other
// way to say "this slab is unknown" through the existing fill path is to upload
// 2 MiB of brick bits that are all ones. See that kernel's own note.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TOROIDAL ADDRESSING (contract item 4)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Regions arrive in LOCAL (window) coordinates and are stored at
// (local + FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel) mod Dim, so the volume is a rolling
// window and a recentre invalidates only the entering slab instead of all
// 16 MiB. The reader mirrors this in VoxelFluidCollision.ush and the CPU
// reference in voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h defines it; all three have to agree
// or the in-editor verify gate (voxel.Fluid.Occupancy.Verify) says so.
//
// The one thing the wrap could have broken here is the whole-word write this
// kernel is built on. It does not, and the reason is an alignment invariant the
// host checks before every dispatch: the wrap offset is always a multiple of 64
// voxels (the recentre quantum) and a region's x is always 32-aligned, so a
// local 32-voxel word column maps to exactly one storage word column and never
// straddles the seam. Without that, one word per region would need a
// read-modify-write and two regions could race on it -- the same hazard the
// snapping rule above exists to remove, arriving by a different door.

#include "/Engine/Public/Platform.ush"
#include "/VoxelEarth/VoxelFluidContract.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Bindings
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The volume, in its write mode. Same name as VoxelFluidCollision.ush's
// read-only declaration and deliberately so -- one name, one meaning. The two
// declarations can never meet: nothing includes both files.
RWBuffer<uint> FluidOccupancyBits;

// The host's upload: every brick of this region, packed x-fastest inside each
// brick (vxc::fluidBrickBitIndex == Brick<8>::cellIndex) and x-fastest across
// bricks, 16 words each. Structured rather than typed because it is a plain
// linear array the CPU writes once per dispatch.
StructuredBuffer<uint> FluidRegionBrickBits;

// 512. From the contract; a uniform rather than a literal so a smaller volume
// is a host change. Must be a power of two -- the wrap masks.
uint FluidVolumeDimVoxels;

// TOROIDAL: the STORAGE coordinate of local (0,0,0). Same name, same meaning
// and same host binding as VoxelFluidCollision.ush's declaration -- one name,
// one meaning, two access modes, and the two declarations can never meet.
uint3 FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel;

// The region, in VOLUME-LOCAL voxels. Min is 32-aligned in x and 8-aligned in
// y/z; Size likewise. Both guaranteed by vxc::fluidSnapRegion on the host and
// re-checked by FVoxelFluidOccupancyVolume::UpdateRegion before dispatch.
uint3 FluidRegionMinVoxel;
uint3 FluidRegionSizeVoxels;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Addressing, shared by both writer kernels
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// LOCAL voxel -> the index of the word that holds it. x-major: 32 voxels per
// word along x, rows along x, then y, then z, over STORAGE coordinates.
// Identical to vxc::fluidVolumeWordIndexLocal, and the ONLY place either kernel
// below computes an address.
uint FluidVolumeWordIndexLocal(uint3 localVoxel)
{
	// lint-shader-ub: allow UNSIGNED_UNDERFLOW - FluidVolumeDimVoxels is the volume edge, uploaded by the host as vxc::kFluidVolumeDimVoxels (512) and static_asserted to be a positive power of two in both VoxelFluidOccupancy.cpp and fluidoccupancy.h; it is never 0, so Dim-1 is the wrap mask 511 and cannot underflow
	const uint3 s = (localVoxel + FluidVolumeWrapOffsetVoxel) & (FluidVolumeDimVoxels - 1u);
	const uint wordsPerRow = FluidVolumeDimVoxels >> 5u;
	return (s.z * FluidVolumeDimVoxels + s.y) * wordsPerRow + (s.x >> 5u);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FluidOccupancyFillMain
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE THREAD PER OUTPUT WORD. Each gathers the four brick rows that share it.
//
// Thread mapping: x = the region's 32-voxel word column, y = region-local
// voxel y, z = region-local voxel z (from the dispatch's z dimension, since
// the group is flat in z). 8x8x1 rather than a flat 64x1x1 because a flat
// mapping over a full-volume rebuild needs 4,194,304/64 = 65,536 groups in one
// dimension and the D3D12 per-dimension dispatch limit is 65,535 -- an
// off-by-one that would only ever fire on the largest possible region.
//
// Mirror of vxc::fluidFillRegion / vxc::fluidRegionWordFromBricks.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void FluidOccupancyFillMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint regionWordsX = FluidRegionSizeVoxels.x >> 5u;   // 32 voxels/word
	if (tid.x >= regionWordsX || tid.y >= FluidRegionSizeVoxels.y ||
	    tid.z >= FluidRegionSizeVoxels.z)
	{
		// The group size rounds up on x and y, so the tail threads of the last
		// group are real and must not write. This is also the bound that makes
		// the store below provably in range.
		return;
	}

	const uint regionBricksX = FluidRegionSizeVoxels.x >> 3u;  // 8 voxels/brick
	const uint regionBricksY = FluidRegionSizeVoxels.y >> 3u;

	const uint iby = tid.y >> 3u, by = tid.y & 7u;
	const uint ibz = tid.z >> 3u, bz = tid.z & 7u;

	// A brick's x-row is 8 CONSECUTIVE bits, because bx is the fastest term of
	// the brick bit index. So a row is one shifted byte, not eight bit tests.
	// (fluidBrickBitIndex(0, by, bz) = 8*by + 64*bz.)
	const uint rowBit = (by << 3u) + (bz << 6u);
	const uint rowWord = rowBit >> 5u;
	const uint rowShift = rowBit & 31u;

	uint packed = 0u;
	[unroll]
	for (uint k = 0u; k < 4u; ++k)   // four bricks share one 32-voxel word
	{
		const uint ibx = tid.x * 4u + k;
		const uint brickBase = (ibx + regionBricksX * (iby + regionBricksY * ibz)) * 16u;
		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - rowShift is `rowBit & 31u` a few lines above, so it is provably in 0..31, below the operand's 32-bit width
		const uint row = (FluidRegionBrickBits[brickBase + rowWord] >> rowShift) & 0xFFu;
		// lint-shader-ub: allow VARIABLE_SHIFT - k is the [unroll]ed counter of a loop with a literal bound of 4, so the distance is 0, 8, 16 or 24
		packed |= row << (k * 8u);
	}

	const uint3 lv = FluidRegionMinVoxel + uint3(tid.x * 32u, tid.y, tid.z);
	// lint-shader-ub: allow UNGUARDED_WRITE - index is bounded by construction: the entry guard caps tid inside the region, and the host refuses any region not wholly inside the volume (FVoxelFluidOccupancyVolume::UpdateRegion, via vxc::fluidRegionInBounds), so lv is below FluidVolumeDimVoxels on every axis; the wrap in FluidVolumeWordIndexLocal is a mask by Dim-1 and cannot leave that range, so the index is below wordsPerRow*Dim*Dim, which is the buffer's exact size
	FluidOccupancyBits[FluidVolumeWordIndexLocal(lv)] = packed;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FluidOccupancyMarkUnbuiltMain
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Marks one region UNBUILT -- all bits solid -- with no upload. The recentre
// path's other half: when the window slides, the entering slab's storage words
// still hold the terrain of the slab that just left, 51.2 m away and entirely
// plausible. Marking is what makes the gap "we do not know yet" (water freezes,
// the safe failure) instead of "here is some terrain from over there" (water
// collides against geometry that is not in front of it, which reads as
// worldgen).
//
// WHY A KERNEL, given this file's own rule that a constant-store kernel is a
// second mechanism for a clear. That rule is about the WHOLE-volume clear,
// where RDG already has AddClearUAVPass. There is no ranged buffer clear, and
// the alternative inside the existing path is uploading 2 MiB of brick bits per
// recentre that are all ones. This shares FluidVolumeWordIndexLocal with the
// fill above, so it is a second ENTRY POINT over one addressing definition, not
// a second definition.
//
// Same thread mapping and same guard as the fill, deliberately: one region
// shape, one dispatch calculation, one off-by-one to get wrong instead of two.
// Mirror of vxc::fluidMarkRegionUnbuilt.
[numthreads(8, 8, 1)]
void FluidOccupancyMarkUnbuiltMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint regionWordsX = FluidRegionSizeVoxels.x >> 5u;
	if (tid.x >= regionWordsX || tid.y >= FluidRegionSizeVoxels.y ||
	    tid.z >= FluidRegionSizeVoxels.z)
	{
		return;
	}

	const uint3 lv = FluidRegionMinVoxel + uint3(tid.x * 32u, tid.y, tid.z);
	// lint-shader-ub: allow UNGUARDED_WRITE - identical bound to FluidOccupancyFillMain's store: the entry guard caps tid inside the region and the host refuses any region not wholly inside the volume, so the wrapped index is below the buffer's exact size
	FluidOccupancyBits[FluidVolumeWordIndexLocal(lv)] = 0xFFFFFFFFu;   // vxc::kFluidVolumeUnbuiltWord
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FluidRebaseParticlesMain -- contract item 8
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHY A PARTICLE KERNEL IS IN THE OCCUPANCY FILE, WHICH IS NOT THE SOLVER'S.
// Stated plainly because it is a boundary crossing and boundary crossings rot
// quietly: VoxelFluidSim.usf is the solver's file and this pass is not the
// solver's business, it is the volume's. Recentring is a property of the
// occupancy window -- the window decides when it moves and by how much -- and
// particle positions are expressed relative to that window's corner by contract
// item 1. So the fix-up belongs with the thing that caused it. The alternative,
// a kernel in VoxelFluidSim.usf, would put a pass the solver never calls from
// its own tick into the solver's file and give two owners one edit.
//
// It writes ParticlesRW through the CONTRACT binding (VoxelFluidContract.ush
// :54-59), by that exact name, so there is no second particle layout here and
// nothing to drift. It is the only write to particle state outside the solver,
// and it happens between solver ticks -- never inside one.
//
// WHAT IT DOES, AND WHY THAT IS THE WHOLE JOB. Positions are origin-relative
// (contract item 1), so moving the origin by D voxels means every stored
// position must lose D*10 UU or the water teleports 51.2 m sideways relative to
// the terrain. Velocity is unchanged: this is a translation of the frame, not a
// boost. Only P0.xyz is touched; P0.w (flags) and P1 (velocity, batch id) are
// written back untouched.
//
// AND NOTHING ELSE PERSISTS. The solver's other position buffers
// (OutPositions/InPositions/GridPositions and the whole sorted domain) are RDG
// transients rebuilt from ParticlesRW every tick, so they need no rebase --
// which is also why this pass must not run in the middle of a tick.
//
// PRECISION. D is a whole number of voxels, so the shift is an exact multiple
// of 10 UU; the subtraction is exact whenever the particle moves toward the new
// origin and rounds by at most half a ULP at 5120 UU (2.4 um, one fortieth of
// the collision skin) when it moves away. The derivation and the bound are in
// voxelcore/fluidoccupancy.h (fluidRebaseDeltaUU) and pinned by
// test_fluidoccupancy.cpp.

RWStructuredBuffer<FVoxelFluidParticle> ParticlesRW;

// The origin's motion in UU: (newOriginVoxel - oldOriginVoxel) * 10, SUBTRACTED
// from every position. Accumulated by the host across any recentres that landed
// since the last rebase, so two moves in one frame cost one pass.
float3 FluidRebaseDeltaUU;
// Slots to touch: the sim's allocation high water, not MAX_PARTICLES. A dead
// slot's position is meaningless, so rebasing it is harmless -- but rebasing
// 300k slots when 5k are allocated is not, and the sim already publishes the
// bound (FVoxelFluidSimState::SimSlotBound).
uint FluidRebaseSlotCount;

// 64 rather than the solver's VOXEL_FLUID_GROUP, which is that file's constant
// and not the contract's. A pure streaming pass over 32-byte records; the group
// size is not load-bearing.
[numthreads(64, 1, 1)]
void FluidRebaseParticlesMain(uint3 tid : SV_DispatchThreadID)
{
	const uint i = tid.x;
	if (i >= FluidRebaseSlotCount)
	{
		return;
	}
	// i is below FluidRebaseSlotCount by the guard above, and the host clamps
	// that to the particle buffer's element count before dispatch
	// (FVoxelFluidOccupancyVolume::AddRebaseParticlesPass), so the store is in
	// range. Only P0.xyz moves: P0.w is the flags word and P1 is velocity plus
	// the spawn batch id, and a frame translation changes neither.
	const float4 p0 = ParticlesRW[i].P0;
	ParticlesRW[i].P0 = float4(p0.xyz - FluidRebaseDeltaUU, p0.w);
}
